From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:19:54 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: give a linear scanout buffer a linear gbm bo

virglrenderer 1.3.0 allocates a resource as a gbm bo only when the
guest negotiated the gbm-layout feature; otherwise it is a GL texture,
whose layout is the driver's choice (BROADCOM_UIF on the Raspberry Pi
5's V3D). A guest that asks for a linear scanout buffer
(VIRGL_BIND_LINEAR | VIRGL_BIND_SCANOUT, which Mesa's virgl driver
sends with the AGL layer's mesa 0004) puts it on a display plane, and
the host compositor can take it from there only if it is linear.

Allocate such resources as gbm bos even without gbm-layout. Both bits
are required, so a resource that is only linear -- a texture the CPU
writes -- stays a GL texture. There is a gbm device only when the
device model runs with VIRGL_EXTERNAL_GBM=1: DomA's qemu does in the
Venus lane, DomU's does with overlay planes (xt-xen-cfg-domu).

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
@@ -8622,7 +8622,15 @@
     * GBM allocation may be less optimal compared to a regular GL allocation.
     * Skip GBM allocation when we don't actually need it.
     */
-   if (!vrend_state.gbm_layout_feat)
+   /*
+    * Unless the guest asked for a linear scanout buffer: one it hands to a
+    * display plane needs that layout, and a GBM bo is the only way to get it
+    * (a GL texture's layout is the driver's choice, tiled on V3D). Both bits,
+    * so a buffer that is only linear (a CPU-written texture) stays as it was.
+    */
+   if (!vrend_state.gbm_layout_feat &&
+       (gr->base.bind & (VIRGL_BIND_LINEAR | VIRGL_BIND_SCANOUT)) !=
+          (VIRGL_BIND_LINEAR | VIRGL_BIND_SCANOUT))
       GBM_INIT_SKIP("GBM layout feature not negotiated (1.3.0 skips GBM then)");
 
    /*
